<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Naucratis</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Naukratis</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Naucratis was a Greek trading city in the western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile-delta">Nile Delta</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, founded in the 7th century BCE. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharaoh">Pharaoh</a> Amasis in the 6th century it became the only port where Greek merchants were allowed to trade, and cities such as Miletus, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samos">Samos</a>, Aegina, Chios and Mytilene kept sanctuaries there, several of them sharing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hellenion">Hellenion</a>. It was a key channel for exchange between Egypt and the Greek world.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/naucratis.jpg" alt="Naucratis" width="320" height="304" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Plate_sphinx_BM_GR1965.9-30.705.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Naucratis stood on the Canopic, westernmost, branch of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a> in the western Delta, about 16 kilometres from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sais">Sais</a>. It was an Egyptian town before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> came, and later a military settlement occupied by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenaries</a>. The Canopic branch was one of the main waterways linking the Nile valley with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a>, and the most accessible in the Saite period. The early settlement grew into a busy trading port in which Greek traders settled and a large Greek community developed.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a> wrote that Amasis, who was partial to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a>, gave the city of Naucratis to those who wished to settle in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>. Amasis, who defeated the pharaoh <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apries">Apries</a> in 570 BCE, closed the military camps of Greek mercenaries and moved the soldiers to Memphis. Archaeologists have proposed an earlier date of about 625 BCE for the first settlement on the site. Amasis turned it into a treaty port, probably to keep Greek activity in one place under his control; it was not the colony of any one city but an open emporion, like Al Mina in north <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>According to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a>, the walled shrine called the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hellenion">Hellenion</a> was a joint undertaking of nine Greek cities: Chios, Klazomenai, Teos and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phocaea">Phocaea</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ionia">Ionia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhodes">Rhodes</a>, Halicarnassus, Knidos and Phaselis from the Dorian cities, and Mytilene from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aeolis">Aeolia</a>. Miletus, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samos">Samos</a> and Aegina had sanctuaries of their own. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egyptians">Egyptians</a> mostly supplied grain, linen and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papyrus">papyrus</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> mostly paid in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> but also in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timber</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olive-oil">olive oil</a> and wine. The Saite pharaohs drew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenaries</a> from the Greek settlements in the Delta.</p><p>Naucratis was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>'s most important harbour in antiquity until the rise of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a> and the shifting of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a> led to its decline, though Heracleion, also called Thonis, rivalled it as a port from the 6th to the 4th century BCE. Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a> it remained a centre of Greek culture, and it produced several orators of the Second Sophistic in the second and early third centuries CE. The writer Athenaeus came from Naucratis.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Discovery and study</h2><p>Flinders Petrie rediscovered the site and dug there in 1884-1885, followed by Ernest Arthur Gardner in 1899 and David George Hogarth in 1903. They found temple ruins, including the Temples of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hera">Hera</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apollo">Apollo</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/castor-and-pollux">Dioscuri</a>, and possibly the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hellenion">Hellenion</a>, and a Greek Temple of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aphrodite">Aphrodite</a>. Pottery from the site carries some of the earliest known Greek inscriptions, among them early Ionic writing. American archaeologists began the Naucratis Project in 1977; the northern sanctuary area lay under a lake formed by the risen water table, and farmers digging earth for fertiliser had destroyed much of the eastern site. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-museum">British Museum</a> ran a project from 2012 to 2023 that produced an accurate topographic map of the site.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Naucratis” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Chian merchants traded widely: Chios was one of the cities that shared the Hellenion sanctuary at <b>Naucratis</b> in Egypt, and its pottery and coinage, which carried a sphinx beside a wine amphora, travelled across the Aegean.</blockquote><a href="/empire/chios">Chios · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>In Egypt, Milesians were among the Greeks trading at <b>Naucratis</b>, where they had their own sanctuary of Apollo.</blockquote><a href="/empire/miletus">Miletus · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Samos had its own sanctuary of Hera at <b>Naucratis</b> in Egypt, and it founded colonies such as Perinthus on the Propontis, Nagidus and Celenderis in Cilicia and Minoa on Amorgos, while exiles from the island settled Dicaearchia, the later Puteoli, in Italy.</blockquote><a href="/empire/samos">Samos · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The archaeological study of the now-submerged Egyptian city of Heracleion, and the recovery of the so-called <b>Naucratis</b> stela, support his claim that Heracleion was founded during the Egyptian New Kingdom.</blockquote><a href="/work/histories-of-herodotus">Histories of Herodotus · Survival &amp; Rediscovery</a></li><li><blockquote>Some lived in its three Greek cities, Alexandria, <b>Naucratis</b> and Ptolemais; most were soldiers settled on land grants (kleroi) across the country, the cleruchs, who farmed in peacetime and could be called to war at any time.</blockquote><a href="/societies/ptolemaic-egypt">Social Hierarchy of Ptolemaic Egypt</a></li><li><blockquote>Its merchants traded at <b>Naucratis</b> in Egypt, and its oligarchic elite built the temple of Aphaia, whose pediment sculptures are now in Munich.</blockquote><a href="/state/aegina">Aegina</a></li></ul></section>
